{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/project_sources.rs",
  "item": "mod[tests]/impl[Project/Domain]/fn[node_path]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Source-tool tests publish OutlineId addresses and retain mapped NodeIds\n directly in dependencies. This required Domain method is unused in\n those flows; stable-path and filesystem-storage tests use other domains."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/project_sources.rs` `mod[tests]/impl[Project/Domain]/fn[node_path]` is valid.\n\n- **Form:** It is a fenced citation with commentary, attached to an explicit function that has a body. The built-in allows that form and role.\n- **Substantive reason:** The commentary explains why execution coverage of this fixture method is acceptable. It says `node_path` is a required `Domain` method that the source-tool flows never call. Those flows publish `OutlineId` addresses and record mapped `NodeId`s straight into `Dependency::Node`. That is a real reason, not just difficulty.\n- **Agreement with source:** The tests in `mod[tests]` of `project_sources.rs` only exercise `read_source`, `list_sources`, `outline_source`, `search_text`, `search_structure` and paging. They also exercise `refresh_evidence` with a deterministic criterion, `inspection.read_source` and `record_is_current`. None of these reaches `node_path`.\n- **Callers:** A search of the crate for `node_path` finds only these callers:\n  - `edge_fingerprint` in `graph.rs`;\n  - the graph-tool paths in `agent/tools.rs`: `ToolAgent::run`, `GraphSession::node` and `GraphSession::dispatch`.\n\n  Those are edge and graph-agent flows. The `Project` fixture's `outgoing_edges` returns an empty vec and carries its own coverage commentary. It says graph-selection regressions use other fixtures.\n- **Other domains:** The crate has other `Domain` fixtures that implement `node_path`, in `agent/tests.rs` and `agent/configured.rs`. This fits the claim that other tests use other domains. I did not trace the stable-path and filesystem-storage tests themselves.\n\nNothing in the citation conflicts with the built-in's criteria, and I found no gap.",
